---
// A cheese as old as Homer, a name from Italian, and a war over who may use it.
---

<p>
	Greece is famously protective of its feta. When Denmark, Germany and France started making feta in the 20th century, and made more than Greece, and made it from cow's milk, Greece went to court.
</p>
<p>
	In 2005 the European Court of Justice ruled in favour of Greece: only cheese from Greece, made from sheep's milk with at most 30% goat's milk, may be called feta.
</p>
<p>
	The US, not bound to the EU's DOP regulations, still makes and sells feta from cow's milk, though it might be running into export problems soon:
</p>
<p>
	With the signing of the EU-Mercosur trade agreement, Argentina, Brazil, Paraguay and Uruguay agree to protect 344 EU food names since May 1st 2026, including feta. This means that these countries may no longer sell US feta, at least not under the name "feta".
</p>
